| footer            = Cinematographer [[Erik Messerschmidt]] took over from [[Greig Fraser]] after he could not return due to scheduling conflicts,<ref name="Messerschmidt" /> while filmmaker [[Steven Soderbergh]] approached Reeves to help direct the remainder of principal photography while he focused on a personal family matter.<ref name="Soderbergh" />
}}
[[Second unit]] filming and a technical [[Recce (filmmaking)|recce]] took place at the old [[Birkenhead Tunnel]] and [[St George's Hall, Liverpool]], starting on May 20, 2026, featuring vehicles with GCPD decals and yellow taxi cabs.<ref name="LiverpoolSecondUnitFilming" /><ref name="LiverpoolRecce" /> [[Principal photography]] commenced on June 12,<ref name="FilmingStart" /> following multiple delays while Reeves meticulously worked on the script.<ref name="ScriptCompleteTheWrap" /><ref name="ReevesSep2025THR" /> Filming is primarily taking place on sound stages at [[Warner Bros. Studios Leavesden]],<ref name="FilmingLocationsSD" /> with cinematographer [[Erik Messerschmidt]],<ref name="Messerschmidt" /> while exterior [[location shooting]] occurs throughout London and [[Liverpool]], England,<ref name="FilmingLocationsSD" /> as well as in [[Glasgow]], Scotland.<ref name="GlasgowFilming" /> The production uses the [[working title]] ''Semper Vigilans'', which was believed to be a reference to the [[Court of Owls]].<ref name="WorkingTitle" /><ref name="LondonFilmingOct2026" /> Eleven weeks of filming were scheduled to take place at night.<ref name="PattinsonNightFilming" /> Farrell was set to film his scenes during the first two weeks in July.<ref name="FarrellScenesSchedule" />

In the middle of July, the release date was further delayed to February 18, 2028, to allow more time for the post-production process.<ref name="Feb2028Date" /> Filming took place around the old Birkenhead Tunnel and within the [[Liverpool City Region]] during the week of July 20,<ref name="LiverpoolMerseysideFilming" /><ref name="LiverpoolRuncornFilming" /> before shooting on the [[Silver Jubilee Bridge]] in [[Runcorn]], [[Cheshire]].<ref name="LiverpoolMerseysideFilming" /><ref name="GlasgowJulyFilming" /><ref name="LiverpoolRuncornFilming" /> Three weeks of filming commenced on August 18 in the [[Glasgow city centre]], taking place at the [[Broomielaw]], the [[Glasgow Bridge, Glasgow|Glasgow Bridge]], the [[Kingston Bridge, Glasgow|Kingston Bridge]], [[North Street, Glasgow|North Street]], and the [[National Cycle Route 75]] along the [[River Clyde]].<ref name="GlasgowJulyFilming" /> 23 bridge pillar murals painted through the arts venue [[SWG3]] and funded by the [[Glasgow City Council]] were covered for filming, with the production expected to coordinate with SWG3 and cover the costs for their reinstatement after filming concluded<ref name="GlasgowMurals" /> In September, filming took place at [[St Paul's Cathedral]] to stand in for the Gotham Bar Association and street protest scenes, and at the [[Bank tube station]] for the Gotham Transit Authority.<ref name="StPaulFilming" /><ref name="LondonFilmingOct2026" />

In early October, Warner Bros. and DC Studios announced that filming had been temporarily halted while Reeves stepped away from on-set physical production to focus on a personal family matter.<ref name="FilmingPause" /> Filmmaker [[Steven Soderbergh]], who admired Reeves's work on ''The Batman'', subsequently approached Reeves about helping him complete work on the film, and he was hired later that month to direct the remaining weeks of principal photography. The pair would closely collaborate to ensure Soderbergh's new footage could be seamlessly integrated with the previously filmed material. Reeves was expected to oversee post-production once he was ready.<ref name="Soderbergh" /><ref name="LondonFilmingOct2026" /> Second unit filming had continued by that point, and filming was set to take place the following week at [[Central Hall Westminster]] near [[Parliament Square]] in London.<ref name="LondonFilmingOct2026" /> In November, Serkis said that he would start filming his scenes after Christmas,<ref name="SerkisFilmingExtended" /> with filming extended into early 2027, due to the continued production delays.<ref name="SerkisFilmingExtended" /><ref name="PattinsonFutureTHR" />

== Music ==
Reeves confirmed in July 2026 that he intended for [[Michael Giacchino]] to return from ''The Batman'' to compose the score for ''Part II''.<ref name="GiacchinoReturn" />

== Marketing ==
On May 7, 2026, Reeves released two camera test photos featuring the Batmobile in winter conditions. Casey Loving at ''[[TheWrap]]'' highlighted the change in setting from ''The Batman'', which he likened to the Christmas setting of ''[[Batman Returns]]'' (1992), and noted the Batmobile's design upgrades.<ref name="ReevesCameraTestWrap" /> Reeves engaged with fans responding to the photos and reposted [[Tony S. Daniel]] and Tomeu Morey's cover art for the "Beasts of Burden" storyline from [[Batman (comic book)#Volume 3|''Batman'' vol. 3]] #57, depicting Batman trekking through snow and covered in blood. That storyline features the [[KGBeast]] and the apparent death of Batman's sidekick, [[Dick Grayson]]. The winter setting and comic art led to speculation that the characters [[Mr. Freeze]] and [[Robin (character)|Robin]] could appear.<ref name="ReevesCameraTestWrap" /><ref name="ReevesCameraTestCommentary" /> Kennedy French of ''[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]'' stated that while the context of the test photos "remain[ed] murky", they made it "clear that the long-gestating sequel is finally moving [forward]".<ref name="ReevesCameraTestVariety" /> Over the course of two days later that month, Reeves announced the cast members through various social media posts, featuring [[GIF]]s of the returning actors from ''The Batman'' and of the new cast using footage from some of their earlier film appearances.<ref name="ReevesConfirmsCastConsequence" /><ref name="ReevesConfirmsCast" /><ref name="ReevesConfirmsCastGizmodo" /> The social media rollout drew comparisons to the similarly hours-long [[Marketing of Avengers: Doomsday#Cast livestream and 2025 conventions|cast announcement livestream]] for the MCU film ''[[Avengers: Doomsday]]'' (2026),<ref name="ReevesConfirmsCast" /><ref name="ReevesConfirmsCastGizmodo" /><ref name="ReevesConfirmsCastGQ" /> though William Goodman at ''[[GQ]]'' felt it was "less arduous".<ref name="ReevesConfirmsCastGQ" /> Nick Romano at ''[[Entertainment Weekly]]'' noted that the use of GIFs was a "very Reeves move, coming with individually wrapped notes for each star".<ref name="ReevesConfirmsCast" /> Justin Carter at ''[[Gizmodo]]'' felt Reeves's "more down-to-earth approach" allowed him to directly communicate with fans while alleviating concerns about the film's long development.<ref name="ReevesConfirmsCastGizmodo" /> ''[[Consequence (magazine)|Consequence]]''{{'s}} Ben Kaye speculated that Reeves's GIF choices indicated which roles the actors were portraying.<ref name="ReevesConfirmsCastConsequence" /> When the delayed February 2028 release date was announced in July, Reeves released a camera test video with a first look at Pattinson in the film's [[Batsuit]]. Loving again highlighted the winter aesthetics.<ref name="ReevesBatsuitFirstLookWrap" />

== Release ==
''The Batman: Part II'' is scheduled to be theatrically released by [[Warner Bros. Pictures]] in the United States on February 18, 2028, in [[IMAX]],<ref name="Feb2028Date" /> as part of the "[[DC Elseworlds]]" label.<ref name="Oct2025ReleaseElseworlds" /> The film was originally scheduled for release on October 3, 2025,<ref name="Oct2025ReleaseElseworlds" /> but was delayed multiple times. After an initial delay to October 2, 2026, due to the [[2023 Hollywood strikes]],<ref name="Oct2026Release" /> it was further shifted to October 1, 2027, to allow more time for writing,<ref name="Oct2027ReleaseTHR" /><ref name="Oct2027ReleaseDelayReason" /> before settling on the February 2028 date to allocate additional time during post-production.<ref name="Feb2028Date" />

== Future ==
In September 2024, Reeves reiterated his earlier plans to develop a ''The Batman'' film trilogy.<ref name="ReevesTrilogySep2024" /> After Pattinson was cast as [[Portrayal of James Bond in film#Robert Pattinson: 2026–present|James Bond]] for the film ''[[Bond 26]]'' (2028) in November 2026, ''TheWrap'' reported that ''Part II''{{'s}} prolonged production had allowed him to exit early from his contract, which committed him to a third and final ''The Batman'' film.<ref name="PattinsonContractNov2026" /> This meant Pattinson could commit to three ''Bond'' films that he was reportedly obligated to.<ref name="PattinsonContractNov2026" /><ref name="PattinsonFutureTHR" /> Writing for ''[[The Hollywood Reporter]]'', [[James Hibberd (writer)|James Hibberd]] reported that some industry observers were skeptical about a third ''The Batman'' film eventuating due to ''Part II''{{'s}} extensive schedule and depending on Pattinson's involvement, while others believed Pattinson could still work around his Bond commitments because his frequently busy schedule was often divided between independent [[arthouse film]]s and major studio [[tentpole]]s. Hibberd noted that some commentators expected then-incoming DC Studios president [[Jeremy Latcham]] to prioritize casting a different actor for the DCU version who could be available for further standalone films and [[Crossover (fiction)|crossovers]], rather than integrating Pattinson's portrayal. Hibberd concluded that Pattinson was unlikely to simultaneously portray both title roles.<ref name="PattinsonFutureTHR" /> Conversely, Ben Child at ''[[The Guardian]]'' believed Pattinson could continue playing Batman alongside Bond and compared this to [[Harrison Ford]] concurrently portraying [[Han Solo]] in the ''[[Star Wars]]'' films (1977–2019) and the [[Indiana Jones (character)|title role]] in the ''[[Indiana Jones]]'' film series (1981–2023). However, while Child noted that the character had been reinvented by numerous directors with their own vision over the years while remaining largely standalone, he opined that Skydance and Latcham could determine that maintaining a Batman portrayal separate from their attempts to rapidly expand the DC shared universe would be an "unnecessary complication" because some believed the character's grounded and gritty approach made him "apparently incompatible" with the wider franchise. He questioned whether integrating Pattinson's Batman into the DCU would "risk puncturing the film's carefully constructed atmosphere". Child concluded, "It would be ironic if Warner Bros ended up sacrificing one of its few genuinely distinctive superhero franchises just as the wider DC universe was beginning to find its feet."<ref name="PattinsonFutureGuardian" /> Sneider subsequently reported that ''Part II'' was expected to be Reeves and Pattinson's last Batman film.<ref name="PattinsonFutureSneider" />

